 \begin{abstract}
-\noindent Answer-engine answers may draw on an organisation's own pages, third-party pages, or information already held by a model. Study~1 audits potential obstacles on first-party access routes, not exclusion from answers. In a purposive sample of organisational websites, \BarAny\% had at least one measured potential obstacle (\BarNoEdge\% when research-client edge refusals are omitted). This composite is not an estimate of access probability or answer visibility; a separate rank-stratified Tranco sample is described by band, without population weights. Study~2 tests specific page-intervention packages for fictional brands in hosted API models, first with pages supplied and then in a searchable-corpus task. With pages supplied, a third-party recommendation produced the largest pooled mention effect among the individual page and guide interventions; evidence and keyword packages also changed mentions. These are not isolated tests of truth, provenance or formatting. In the searchable-corpus task, search compliance, snippet exposure and page opening are reported separately; its contrast with supplied pages also changes the candidate pool and tool regime, so it does not isolate retrieval as a mediator. The longer-page condition uses synthetic text, not realistic webpages. In an exploratory interface audit, we observed no visible text-search activity or source citations in \AudCNQW{} sessions; this does not establish that answers came solely from parametric memory. The paper provides a website measurement and a controlled benchmark of context-dependent content effects, not estimates of production-engine returns or remediation effects.
+\noindent Answer engines such as ChatGPT and Perplexity answer questions by naming a few organisations and citing
+sources. We study two conditions for appearing in them: whether engines' crawlers can reach a site's own pages,
+and how page content affects selection. Study~1 audits \NSites{} organisational websites in \NSectors{} sectors and
+\RandSites{} popular domains from four Tranco rank bands. Of the organisational sites, \BarAny\% showed at least one
+potential obstacle on a first-party crawling route (\BarNoEdge\% without research-client refusals at the network
+edge), mostly not expressed in agent-specific robots.txt rules, and sites restricted AI training crawlers far more
+often than answer agents; these are measured obstacles, not demonstrated exclusion. Study~2 is a
+pre-specified experiment with fictional brands: 14 page interventions in 12 hosted API models, with a follow-up on
+\FlNModelsTwo{} more. With the pages in the model's context, a third party's recommendation raised the chance that a
+brand was named by \EMenAllThirdPartyU{} percentage points, more than any edit to the brand's own page; citation and
+statistics packages added \EMenAllCitationsU{} and \EMenAllStatisticsU{} points, keyword stuffing also helped, and an
+instruction embedded in the page worked on most models but not on Anthropic's. When \AgNModelsW{} models searched a
+synthetic corpus themselves, the brand was named in \AgBaseMen\% of baseline answers instead of \AgBaseMenCtx\%,
+evidence and instruction effects were no longer detectable, and text matching the question made pages easier to find.
+In a snapshot audit, ChatGPT's free tier showed no visible search or citations. We release the toolkit, benchmark and
+data.
