-This paper measures potential obstacles to first-party crawler access and tests how specific content packages affect brand mentions in controlled answer tasks. The website audit does not measure actual exclusion from answers, and its composite is not an access probability. In experiments with fictional brands, intervention effects depended on whether pages were supplied or had to be found in a searchable synthetic corpus; the two regimes differ in more than retrieval, and neither estimates production-engine returns. Evidence packages, third-party recommendations and query-matching text had different effects across conditions, while the longer-page comparison used synthetic text. A limited interface audit observed no visible text-search activity or source citations in the recorded ChatGPT sessions, but cannot establish how the answers were generated. These findings support treating crawler policy, research-client accessibility, text availability, retrieval and answer selection as distinct measurement targets. Estimating whether remediation changes visibility, and how these results transfer to deployed answer engines, requires direct before-and-after access and visibility measurements and experiments with matched retrieval conditions.
+Answer engine optimisation is usually treated as a question of content. Our results suggest that access deserves
+equal attention. About a third of prominent organisations' websites, and a fifth if research-client refusals at the
+network edge are set aside, showed at least one potential obstacle on a first-party crawling route, and most of these
+were not expressed through an agent-specific robots.txt rule. These are measured conditions, not demonstrated exclusion
+from answers. When the pages were supplied to the model, a third party's recommendation did more than any edit to the
+brand's own page, pooled over 12 hosted API models and again over \FlNModelsTwo{} additional ones. Citation and
+statistics packages were the best edits to the page and the content answers repeated most, but keyword stuffing also
+helped, and on longer synthetic pages saying plainly which questions a page answers mattered as much as endorsement.
+When models had to search a corpus for the pages themselves, being found came first: evidence packages no longer had a
+detectable effect, text that matched the question made a page easier to find, and endorsement still helped. An
+instruction embedded in page text worked on most models, which is a problem for engine operators to fix rather than a
+technique for organisations to use. The interface audit sets a limit on all of this: ChatGPT's free tier showed no
+visible retrieval, so for the most used assistant what earlier crawls and training captured may matter as much as the
+live page. Our search task used a small synthetic corpus, and none of these interventions was tested on a deployed
+engine; the released harness can run the same protocol on production engines, and that is the next test of these
+results.
